        <ExpandableItem summary="TiptapEditorWrapped.tsx — wrapCompound config (61 lines)">
          <Markdown><![CDATA[
```typescript
import { TiptapEditorRender } from "./TiptapEditorRender";
import { wrapCompound } from "../../components-core/wrapComponent";
import {
  createMetadata,
  d,
  dDidChange,
  dInitialValue,
} from "../metadata-helpers";

const COMP = "TiptapEditor";

export const TiptapEditorMd = createMetadata({
  status: "experimental",
  description:
    "`TiptapEditor` wraps the Tiptap rich-text editor as an XMLUI component. " +
    "It provides a full-featured markdown editing experience with toolbar, " +
    "table editing, task lists, and live markdown output — all via `wrapCompound`.",
  props: {
    initialValue: dInitialValue(),
    placeholder: d("Placeholder text shown when the editor is empty.", undefined, "string", "Start writing..."),
    editable: d("Whether the editor is editable.", undefined, "boolean", true),
    toolbar: d("Whether to show the formatting toolbar.", undefined, "boolean", true),
    toolbarItems: d("Comma-separated list of toolbar items to show (e.g. 'bold,italic,bulletList,link,undo,redo'). If omitted, all items are shown.", undefined, "string"),
    height: d("The height of the editor content area.", undefined, "string", "300px"),
  },
  events: {
    didChange: dDidChange(COMP),
  },
  apis: {
    focus: {
      description: "Sets focus on the editor.",
      signature: "focus(): void",
    },
    setValue: {
      description: "Sets the editor content (markdown string).",
      signature: "setValue(value: string): void",
      parameters: { value: "The new markdown content." },
    },
    getMarkdown: {
      description: "Gets the current editor content as markdown.",
      signature: "getMarkdown(): string",
    },
    getHTML: {
      description: "Gets the current editor content as HTML.",
      signature: "getHTML(): string",
    },
  },
});

export const tiptapEditorComponentRenderer = wrapCompound(
  COMP,
  TiptapEditorRender,
  TiptapEditorMd,
  {
    booleans: ["editable", "toolbar"],
    strings: ["placeholder", "height", "toolbarItems"],
    events: {
      didChange: "onDidChange",
    },
  },
);
```
]]></Markdown>
        </ExpandableItem>

      </VStack>
    </Page>

  <Page url="/gauge">
    <VStack padding="{responsive('0.75rem', '2rem')}" gap="0">

      <H1>Gauge + Theming</H1>

      <Markdown><![CDATA[
So far we've ignored a key aspect of XMLUI: [themes](https://docs.xmlui.org/themes-intro). The SliderW example responds to XMLUI theme settings because it inherits CSS from Slider.

```
import styles from "./Slider.module.scss";
```
]]></Markdown>

<ExpandableItem
  summary="Slider.module.scss">
       <Markdown>
            <![CDATA[
```scss
@use "../../components-core/theming/themes" as t;

// --- This code snippet is required to collect the theme variables used in this module
$themeVars: ();
@function createThemeVar($componentVariable) {
  $themeVars: t.appendThemeVar($themeVars, $componentVariable) !global;
  @return t.getThemeVar($themeVars, $componentVariable);
}

$componentName: "Slider";

// Variables for default variant
$borderRadius-Slider--default: createThemeVar("Input:borderRadius-#{$componentName}--default");
$borderColor-Slider--default: createThemeVar("Input:borderColor-#{$componentName}--default");
$borderWidth-Slider--default: createThemeVar("Input:borderWidth-#{$componentName}--default");
$borderStyle-Slider--default: createThemeVar("Input:borderStyle-#{$componentName}--default");
$boxShadow-Slider--default: createThemeVar("Input:boxShadow-#{$componentName}--default");
// ... more variants (error, warning, success) ...

// Variables for @layer section
$backgroundColor-track-Slider: createThemeVar("Input:backgroundColor-track-#{$componentName}");
$backgroundColor-range-Slider: createThemeVar("Input:backgroundColor-range-#{$componentName}");
$borderWidth-thumb-Slider: createThemeVar("Input:borderWidth-thumb-#{$componentName}");
$backgroundColor-thumb-Slider: createThemeVar("Input:backgroundColor-thumb-#{$componentName}");
// ... more variables ...

@layer components {
  .sliderTrack {
    background-color: $backgroundColor-track-Slider;
    // ...
  }
  .sliderThumb {
    background-color: $backgroundColor-thumb-Slider;
    // ...
  }
}

:export {
  themeVars: t.json-stringify($themeVars);
}
```
]]></Markdown>
</ExpandableItem>

      <Markdown><![CDATA[
We've provided no `.scss` for `Knob`, `Monaco`, and `TipTap`. This isn't something we can just wrap in a function. The SCSS boilerplate is component-specific and largely irreducible. Monaco and TipTap already have rich styling systems. Rather than duplicating them it would make sense to bridge them, for example by passing `--xmlui-color-primary` into Tiptap's toolbar button styles via CSS custom properties, or mapping XMLUI's light/dark mode to Monaco's theme prop.

Let's explore that idea with another library, [Smart UI](https://www.htmlelements.com/). The Smart UI [Gauge](https://www.htmlelements.com/docs/gauge/) can be wrapped using the methods we've already seen -- and we can add an SCSS theming bridge that connects Smart UI's SVG styling to XMLUI's design tokens.

### The problem

Smart UI Gauge renders using its own CSS custom properties (`--smart-background`, `--smart-primary`, etc.) and hardcodes some SVG `fill` attributes inline. Meanwhile, XMLUI's theme system uses CSS custom properties backed by design tokens like `$color-primary` and `$color-surface-50`.

### The bridge

An SCSS module declares three XMLUI theme variables and maps them to Smart UI's own CSS custom properties:

```scss
$backgroundColor-Gauge: createThemeVar("Input:backgroundColor-#{$componentName}");
$textColor-Gauge:       createThemeVar("Input:textColor-#{$componentName}");
$primaryColor-Gauge:    createThemeVar("Input:primaryColor-#{$componentName}");

:global(smart-gauge) {
  --smart-background: #{$backgroundColor-Gauge};
  --smart-primary:    #{$primaryColor-Gauge};
  --smart-surface:    #{$backgroundColor-Gauge};
}
```

The `defaultThemeVars` in the metadata maps these to XMLUI design tokens:

```typescript
defaultThemeVars: {
  "backgroundColor-Gauge": "$color-surface-50",
  "textColor-Gauge":       "$textColor-primary",
  "primaryColor-Gauge":    "$color-primary",
},
```

Result: the Gauge automatically respects the current XMLUI theme and light/dark mode with no theme code in the render component.

Use the theme selector below to switch between the default theme and `earthtone` (defined by just three color values: `color-primary`, `color-secondary`, `color-surface`). Use the tone switch to toggle light/dark mode. Both cascade through the bridge into the Gauge.
]]></Markdown>

      <Card var.gaugeVal="42">
        <HStack alignItems="center" wrapContent="{true}">
          <Gauge
            id="g1"
            minValue="0"
            maxValue="100"
            initialValue="42"
            analogDisplayType="needle"
            digitalDisplay="{true}"
            onDidChange="{(v) => gaugeVal = v}" />
          <VStack>
            <Text>Value: {gaugeVal}</Text>
            <Select
              label="Theme"
              initialValue="xmlui"
              onDidChange="{ (newTheme) => setTheme(newTheme) }"
            >
              <Option value="xmlui" label="default"/>
              <Option value="earthtone" label="earthtone"/>
            </Select>
            <ToneSwitch />
            <HStack gap="0.5rem">
              <Button size="sm" onClick="{g1.setValue(0)}">Set 0</Button>
              <Button size="sm" onClick="{g1.setValue(50)}">Set 50</Button>
              <Button size="sm" onClick="{g1.setValue(100)}">Set 100</Button>
            </HStack>
          </VStack>
        </HStack>
      </Card>

      <Markdown><![CDATA[
### How the theming bridge works

Three pieces connect Smart UI's styling to XMLUI's design tokens:

1. The SCSS module declares XMLUI theme variables and maps them to Smart UI's own CSS custom properties (`--smart-background`, `--smart-primary`, etc.).
2. The `defaultThemeVars` in `GaugeWrapped.tsx` maps each SCSS variable to an XMLUI design token (e.g. `$color-primary`, `$textColor-primary`) that automatically adapts to light/dark mode.
3. The render component (`GaugeRender.tsx`) is pure React -- it imports only Smart UI's Gauge and the SCSS module. It uses `classnames(styles.gaugeContainer, className)` on the outer div, which is what connects the theming bridge to the DOM. No XMLUI imports, no theme logic.

The SCSS bridge also handles responsive layout. Smart UI Gauge renders at a fixed pixel width, which overflows on small screens. Adding `max-width: 100%` and `width: 100% !important` to the `:global(smart-gauge)` selector makes it respect its container's width. This is a case where the bridge goes beyond color theming into layout — the same SCSS module that maps design tokens also constrains the component's sizing behavior.

### The pattern

For any third-party component with CSS-accessible styling:

1. Write an SCSS module that maps XMLUI `createThemeVar` calls to the library's CSS selectors
2. Add `defaultThemeVars` to your `wrapCompound` config, mapping each variable to XMLUI design tokens
3. Apply the SCSS module class in your render component via `classnames`

The `wrapCompound` plumbing handles everything else: state, events, APIs, and trace events -- exactly as shown in the earlier sections.

### The strategy

Consider a line like this from the SCSS module:

```scss
$backgroundColor-Gauge: createThemeVar("Input:backgroundColor-#{$componentName}");
```

The `Input:` prefix places this variable in the Input category of XMLUI's theme variable taxonomy. How did we know to use `Input:` rather than, say, `Display:` or just the bare variable name? And how did we know that `$color-surface-50` and `$textColor-primary` were the right tokens to map to in `defaultThemeVars`?

XMLUI has thousands of theme variables organized into categories. To navigate this vocabulary we used [xmlui-mcp](https://github.com/xmlui-org/xmlui-mcp), a Model Context Protocol server that gives agents access to XMLUI's documentation, component source, and theme variable definitions. When asked "what tokens control background color for input-style components?", the MCP server searched the theme variable defaults and found the `Input:backgroundColor-*` pattern used by Slider, TextBox, and other input components. Similarly, searching for "text color" surfaced `$textColor-primary` (which resolves to `$color-surface-950` and automatically inverts in dark mode).

We also discovered Smart UI's own CSS custom properties (`--smart-background`, `--smart-primary`, etc.) by inspecting the library's source. This led to the key insight: instead of targeting individual SVG class selectors, we could map XMLUI tokens to Smart UI's own theming API, reducing the bridge from ~15 variables to just three.

]]></Markdown>

      <VStack gap="0">
      <ExpandableItem summary="GaugeRender.tsx -- pure React, no XMLUI imports">
        <Markdown><![CDATA[
```typescript
import React, { useCallback, useEffect, useRef } from "react";
import { forwardRef } from "react";
import { Gauge } from "smart-webcomponents-react/gauge";
import "smart-webcomponents-react/source/styles/smart.default.css";
import styles from "./Gauge.module.scss";
import classnames from "classnames";

export const GaugeRender = forwardRef(({
  value, onChange, registerApi, className,
  min = 0, max = 100,
  analogDisplayType = "needle",
  digitalDisplay = false,
  startAngle = -30, endAngle = 210,
  scalePosition = "inside",
  animation = "none",
  unit = "", showUnit = false,
  enabled = true,
  ...rest
}: any, ref: any) => {
  const gaugeRef = useRef<any>(null);
  const numValue = typeof value === "number" ? value : min;

  useEffect(() => {
    registerApi?.({
      focus: () => gaugeRef.current?.focus(),
      setValue: (v: any) => onChange?.(Number(v)),
    });
  }, [registerApi, onChange]);

  const handleChange = useCallback((event: any) => {
    const newVal = event?.detail?.value ?? event?.detail;
    if (newVal != null) onChange(Math.round(Number(newVal) * 100) / 100);
  }, [onChange]);

  return (
    <div ref={ref} className={classnames(styles.gaugeContainer, className)} {...rest}>
      <Gauge
        ref={gaugeRef}
        value={numValue}
        min={min} max={max}
        analogDisplayType={analogDisplayType}
        digitalDisplay={digitalDisplay}
        startAngle={startAngle} endAngle={endAngle}
        scalePosition={scalePosition}
        animation={animation}
        unit={unit} showUnit={showUnit}
        disabled={!enabled}
        onChange={handleChange}
      />
    </div>
  );
});

GaugeRender.displayName = "GaugeRender";
```
]]></Markdown>
      </ExpandableItem>

      <ExpandableItem summary="GaugeWrapped.tsx -- wrapCompound config with theming">
        <Markdown><![CDATA[
```typescript
import { GaugeRender } from "./GaugeRender";
import styles from "./Gauge.module.scss";
import { parseScssVar } from "../../components-core/theming/themeVars";
import { wrapCompound } from "../../components-core/wrapComponent";
import {
  createMetadata, d, dDidChange, dEnabled, dInitialValue,
} from "../metadata-helpers";

const COMP = "Gauge";

export const GaugeMd = createMetadata({
  status: "experimental",
  description: "`Gauge` wraps the Smart UI Gauge web component...",
  props: {
    initialValue: dInitialValue(),
    minValue: { description: "Minimum value.", valueType: "number", defaultValue: 0 },
    maxValue: { description: "Maximum value.", valueType: "number", defaultValue: 100 },
    analogDisplayType: d("Display type: 'needle', 'fill', or 'line'.", undefined, "string", "needle"),
    digitalDisplay: d("Show digital value display.", undefined, "boolean", false),
    // ... more props ...
    enabled: dEnabled(),
  },
  events: { didChange: dDidChange(COMP) },
  apis: {
    setValue: { description: "Sets the gauge value.", signature: "setValue(value: number): void" },
    focus: { description: "Sets focus on the gauge.", signature: "focus(): void" },
  },
  themeVars: parseScssVar(styles.themeVars),
  defaultThemeVars: {
    [`backgroundColor-${COMP}`]: "$color-surface-50",
    [`textColor-${COMP}`]: "$textColor-primary",
    [`primaryColor-${COMP}`]: "$color-primary",
  },
});

export const gaugeComponentRenderer = wrapCompound(COMP, GaugeRender, GaugeMd, {
  booleans: ["enabled", "digitalDisplay", "showUnit"],
  numbers: ["minValue", "maxValue", "startAngle", "endAngle"],
  strings: ["analogDisplayType", "scalePosition", "animation", "unit"],
  events: { didChange: "onDidChange" },
  rename: { minValue: "min", maxValue: "max" },
  parseInitialValue: (raw, props) => {
    const min = Number(props.min) || 0;
    const max = Number(props.max) || 100;
    let val = typeof raw === "string" ? parseFloat(raw) : raw;
    if (val == null || isNaN(val)) val = min;
    return Math.min(max, Math.max(min, Number(val)));
  },
  formatExternalValue: (value, props) => {
    const min = Number(props.min) || 0;
    const max = Number(props.max) || 100;
    const val = Number(value);
    if (isNaN(val)) return min;
    return Math.min(max, Math.max(min, val));
  },
});
```
]]></Markdown>
      </ExpandableItem>
      </VStack>

    </VStack>
  </Page>


  <Page url="/echarts">
    <VStack padding="{responsive('0.75rem', '2rem')}" gap="0">

      <H1>ECharts</H1>

      <Markdown><![CDATA[
The Gauge page showed CSS-level theming: an SCSS module bridges XMLUI design tokens to Smart UI's CSS custom properties. The render component stays pure React.

Apache ECharts takes a different approach to styling. There are no CSS custom properties to bridge to — all colors, fonts, and visual properties live in a JavaScript `option` object passed to the chart. This means the SCSS bridge pattern doesn't apply.

Instead, the render component reads XMLUI design tokens at runtime using `useTheme()` — the same React context hook that 35+ XMLUI components already use — and merges them into the ECharts option. The XMLUI author just passes data; theme colors are injected automatically.

### How it works

The render component builds themed defaults and merges them with the user's option:

```typescript
const { getThemeVar } = useTheme();

const palette = [
  getThemeVar("color-primary-500"),
  getThemeVar("color-success-500"),
  getThemeVar("color-warn-500"),
  // ...same tokens as Recharts charts
].filter(Boolean);

const themedOption = deepMerge({
  color: palette,
  textStyle: { color: getThemeVar("textColor-primary") },
  tooltip: { backgroundColor: getThemeVar("color-surface-50") },
  // axis defaults, grid defaults...
}, userOption);  // user option wins
```

Any color the user specifies explicitly in the option overrides the theme default. This keeps the XMLUI author's API simple while ensuring charts look right in any theme.
]]></Markdown>

